<SiteLayout title="Verification" description="Identity, adulthood and photo checks answer different questions. Swoovr’s approach is to explain the difference." eyebrow="Trust, explained" heading="A check should mean something clear." intro="Identity, adulthood and photo checks answer different questions. Swoovr’s approach is to explain the difference.">
<section class="editorial-split"><h2>Clear meaning.<br />No blanket promises.</h2><div><p>Our product plan includes adulthood and identity checks before live discovery and contact. We’re assessing appropriate verification methods and alternatives, including how people can get help when a check doesn’t work for them.</p><p>The final methods, providers and eligibility rules will be explained before people are asked to use them. Signing up for website updates or beta interest is not an identity check.</p></div></section><section class="verification-guide" aria-labelledby="verification-types">
  <h2 id="verification-types">Two badges. Two different checks.</h2>
  <p class="verification-intro">These are the verification markers in Swoovr’s design. Each describes a specific check, rather than a general endorsement of a person.</p>
  <article class="verification-type">
    <img src="/brand/verification/id-verified.svg" alt="" width="96" height="96" />
    <div><h3>ID verified</h3><p>The identity marker is intended to show that someone has completed Swoovr’s identity and adulthood verification process.</p><p>It concerns the person behind the account. It doesn’t mean every profile photo has been checked, and it doesn’t make their legal name or identity documents public.</p></div>
  </article>
  <article class="verification-type">
    <img src="/brand/verification/photo-verified.svg" alt="" width="96" height="96" />
    <div><h3>Photo verified</h3><p>The photo marker is intended to show that a profile photo has been checked against the person presenting it.</p><p>It concerns the photo’s connection to that person. It doesn’t replace identity or adulthood verification, confirm every detail on a profile, or prove someone’s intentions.</p></div>
  </article>
  <p class="verification-footnote">The checks are separate: completing one should not imply the other. The method and scope of each check will be explained alongside the verification process.</p>
</section>
<section class="reading-section"><h2>What a sign-in tells you</h2><p>Signing in with an email or a social provider is about access to an account. It is separate from ID and photo verification.</p><p>Duplicate-image detection is another signal for review. It does not, by itself, earn a photo-verification badge or establish that an image belongs to someone.</p></section>
<section class="editorial-split"><h2>Less information.<br />A clearer purpose.</h2><div><p>Our design preference is to retain the minimum verification result needed, rather than keeping copies of raw identity documents by default. Provider handling, retention and alternatives must be explained alongside the actual process.</p><p>Please don’t send ID, selfies or verification documents through this website’s signup or contact forms. Those forms are for conversations with the team.</p><p>A completed check cannot guarantee someone’s behaviour, eliminate fraud or replace your own boundaries.</p><a class="text-link" href="/contact/">Ask a verification question ↗</a></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
